  <main><section class="article-hero section-pad"><a class="back-link" href="/#stories">← All research stories</a><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH STORY · ANTARCTICA · PHOTOGRAMMETRY</p><h1>Turning old<br />airphotos into a<br /><em>long view of ice.</em></h1><p class="article-deck">Aerial photographs are more than snapshots. With the right geometry and careful processing, they can help reconstruct glacier surfaces and measure change over time.</p><p class="article-byline">An accessible introduction to research led by Sarah F. Child and collaborators.</p></section>
    <article class="article-body"><p class="article-lead">Long before satellites watched Earth every day, aircraft cameras were recording the polar landscape. Those old images can still answer new questions.</p><p>In East Antarctica, Sarah Child and co-authors investigated how historical aerial photographs could be used to create detailed elevation models. Their study uses structure-from-motion photogrammetry—a method that estimates three-dimensional shape from overlapping photographs—with ground control derived from high-resolution satellite imagery.</p><h2>Making a three-dimensional landscape</h2><p>The team applied the method to overlapping vertical photographs collected over Byrd Glacier in December 1978 and January 1979. Matching shared features across the photographs makes it possible to reconstruct an elevation surface for a period before modern satellite records began.</p><p>They compared the resulting historical surface with newer elevation data. Their analysis found that Byrd Glacier’s surface elevation had remained roughly constant over the approximately 40-year period they studied.</p><aside class="article-callout">Aerial photographs from 1978–79 made it possible to build the oldest, highest-resolution glacier elevation models reported for Antarctica in this study.</aside><h2>Why the details matter</h2><p>Historical images were not necessarily collected for modern change detection. Their coverage, camera information, and ground reference can be uneven. The researchers used satellite-derived control to address a key limitation: traditional methods often require precise camera metadata and extensive measurements collected on the ground, which are difficult to obtain in Antarctica.</p><p>This work connects directly with Sarah’s broader focus on remote sensing. The photographs offer scientific evidence and a compelling visual record—the same kind of imagery that can make landscape change easier to see and communicate.</p><a class="button button-dark" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/rs13010021" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a><p class="citation">Child, S. F. et al. (2021). “Structure-from-Motion Photogrammetry of Antarctic Historical Aerial Photographs in Conjunction with Ground Control Derived from Satellite Data.” <em>Remote Sensing</em>, 13(1), 21. Published online December 23, 2020.</p></article>
